  "summary": "Default blue links, system fonts, zero mercy: websites that refuse to be comfortable.",
  "description": "Web brutalism applies architectural brutalism's honesty to the browser: raw HTML defaults, unstyled blue links, Times New Roman, clashing highlight colors, exposed markup and deliberate user-hostile composition. It emerged in mid-2010s design discourse as a reaction against template sameness (Squarespace gloss) and was adopted strategically — Balenciaga's 2015 site, Craigslist as accidental ancestor, indie portfolios, and the brutalistwebsites.com index documenting hundreds. The aesthetic's politics: content before decoration, structure visible, comfort refused. Its cousin \"neubrutalism\" (2021+) keeps the harsh borders but re-adds poster energy and playful color blocks.",
  "culturalContext": "Web brutalism refuses interface comfort: raw HTML defaults, system fonts and exposed structure — adopted strategically by Balenciaga and indie portfolios as a politics of content-first honesty. It remains design discourse's standing critique of template sameness.",
